MPM idealized iceberg testcases
===============================

Single iceberg tests of the individual terms in the iceberg momentum
equation: air and ocean drag, Coriolis, ocean surface tilt, wave radiation,
sea ice drag and stress forces, sea ice capture, and grounding. Each test
switches on one force (or a small set of forces) with simple uniform forcing
so the result can be compared with an expected, usually analytical, answer.


Running
-------

Requirements:
  * the standalone model built at
    ../../../../MPAS-Seaice-MPM/components/mpas-seaice/seaice_model
  * MPAS_SEAICE_STANDALONE_DATA set to the standalone data directory
    (the spherical grid and graph files are linked from
    $MPAS_SEAICE_STANDALONE_DATA/testcases/strain_stress_divergence)
  * python with numpy, netCDF4, matplotlib and colorama

Run all tests:
  python run_testcase.py

Replot a single test from existing output:
  python plot_results.py -t <testcase>

Each test writes its output to output_<testcase>/ and its plots to
results_<testcase>*.png. The grounding (grnd_*) and sea ice stress
(seaice_stress_*) tests also print a PASS/FAIL line for each check.


Files
-----

run_testcase.py         writes the iceberg, initial condition and generated
                        namelist/streams files, then runs every test
plot_results.py         plotting for all tests
grounding_tests.py      definitions, file generation and checks for the
                        grnd_* tests
seaice_stress_tests.py  definitions, file generation and checks for the
                        seaice_stress_* tests

namelist.seaice.<testcase>, streams.seaice.<testcase>
                        namelist and streams files for the basic tests. The
                        grnd_* files are generated from
                        namelist/streams.seaice.grounding_force and the
                        seaice_stress_* files from
                        namelist/streams.seaice.seaice_force_mid_conc, so
                        these templates must be kept in the repository.


Common setup
------------

  Mesh             2562 cell global sphere (grid.2562.nc)
  Iceberg          one iceberg starting at 45N, 0E (icebergs_midlatitude.nc)
                   length 1000 m, height 1000 m, width 667 m
                   (config_iceberg_initial_length_to_width_ratio = 1.5)
                   density 850 kg/m^3, mass 5.67e11 kg, draft 828.5 m
  Time step        config_dt = 3600 s, momentum velocity update
  Grid             config_rotate_cartesian_grid = false
  Forcing          uniform northward (v) air, ocean or sea ice velocities,
                   so icebergs move northwards along the 0E meridian
  Disabled         rollover, ablation (except where noted), all forces not
                   under test

Below, "north" means the v direction. Values quoted are from the current
model and are the expected results.


Basic force tests
-----------------

air_drag
  Forces:   air drag only. Air velocity 1 m/s north, iceberg initially at rest.
  Expected: the iceberg accelerates northwards towards the 1 m/s air speed.
            Air drag is weak for an iceberg this size, so the approach is
            slow: v = 0.016 m/s after 1 day, 0.33 m/s after 30 days and
            0.75 m/s after 180 days. The iceberg reaches the north pole
            (latitude pi/2) after about 120 days, where longitude jumps to pi
            as it passes over the pole.

ocean_drag
  Forces:   ocean drag only. Ocean velocity 1 m/s north, iceberg at rest,
            4 day run with hourly output.
  Expected: quadratic drag gives the analytical solution
              v(t) = U - U / (1 + k U t),  k = D / M
            with U = 1 m/s, D the ocean drag coefficient and M the iceberg
            mass. The model matches this to 4 decimal places
            (v = 0.788, 0.882, 0.918, 0.937, 0.949 m/s after 1-5 hours),
            reaching 0.997 m/s after 4 days.

coriolis
  Forces:   Coriolis only. Iceberg starts with v = 1 m/s at 45N.
  Expected: an inertial oscillation with period 2 pi / f = 16.9 hours and
            radius |u| / f. The implicit Coriolis treatment damps the speed
            by a factor 1 / sqrt(1 + (f dt)^2) = 0.937 each step, so with
            daily output the speed drops to 0.21 m/s after 1 day and
            effectively zero after a few days. The iceberg stays close to
            its starting position. Plots: trajectory on the sphere and speed.

surface_tilt
  Forces:   ocean surface tilt only. Ocean velocity 1 m/s north, 5 day run
            with hourly output.
  Expected: the geostrophic surface tilt force -M f v_ocean in the u
            direction accelerates the iceberg westwards at a constant rate:
              u(t) = -f v_ocean t
            (u = -0.371, -0.743, -1.114 m/s after 1-3 hours, matching the
            analytical value). Nothing resists this force, so the speed grows
            without bound, reaching 44 m/s westwards after 5 days. The run is
            kept short so the iceberg does not exceed the motion limit.

wave_radiation
  Forces:   wave radiation only. Air velocity 1 m/s north, ocean velocity
            1 m/s south.
  Expected: a constant 396 N northward wave radiation force with no
            resisting force, so v grows linearly: v = F t / M, giving
            0.011 m/s after 180 days.


Sea ice drag and capture tests
------------------------------

These use config_iceberg_seaice_force_type = "drag" (drag on the iceberg
width times the sea ice thickness) and config_iceberg_seaice_capture_type =
"force" (captured if the sea ice force needed to move the iceberg with the
sea ice is no more than pressure * iceberg width). The sea ice thickness is
set through iceVolumeCell = concentration * 1 m. The reaction of the sea ice
force is applied to the sea ice. Plots include the force on the iceberg and
the total force on the sea ice, summed over vertices.

seaice_force_low_conc
  Setup:    sea ice concentration 0.01, sea ice velocity 1 m/s north,
            pressure 0.
  Expected: concentration is below config_iceberg_seaice_concentration_lower_limit
            (0.15), so there is no sea ice force and the iceberg stays at
            rest.

seaice_force_mid_conc
  Setup:    concentration 0.5, sea ice velocity 1 m/s north, pressure 0.
  Expected: the sea ice drag (1.4e5 N after 1 day, decreasing as the
            iceberg speeds up) accelerates the iceberg towards the sea ice
            velocity: v = 0.19, 0.41, 0.68 and 0.81 m/s after 10, 30, 90 and
            180 days. Zero pressure means the iceberg is never captured. The
            force on the sea ice is equal and opposite to the force on the
            iceberg at every step.

seaice_force_high_conc
  Setup:    concentration 0.99, sea ice velocity 0.5 m/s north, ocean
            velocity 0.4 m/s north, pressure 4.5e4 N/m (capture limit
            pressure * width = 3e7 N), ocean drag on, 2 day run with hourly
            output.
  Expected: the transition from uncaptured to captured.
            Hours 1-5: not captured. The sea ice force needed to move the
              iceberg with the sea ice (8.5e7 N falling to 3.1e7 N) exceeds
              the limit, so the iceberg is accelerated by ocean and sea ice
              drag (v = 0.24 to 0.35 m/s).
            Hour 6: captured. The required force (2.9e7 N) falls below the
              limit and the iceberg jumps to the 0.5 m/s sea ice velocity.
            Hours 7-48: stays captured. The sea ice only has to hold the
              iceberg against ocean drag (5.9e6 N).
            results_seaice_force_high_conc_capture.png shows the velocities
            and the implied capture force against the capture limit, with
            the captured period shaded.


Sea ice stress tests (seaice_stress_*)
--------------------------------------

These test config_iceberg_seaice_force_type = "stress". The variational
scheme sea ice stresses stress11var, stress22var and stress12var are read in
from the initial conditions stream as a uniform stress at all cell corners.
The velocity solver is off, so the stresses stay fixed (pkgVariational is
activated for stress icebergs for this). Only the sea ice force is active,
with no capture or grounding, and runs are 1 day with hourly output.

The expected force on the iceberg is
  F = n * min(W sigma_c, M |du| / dt)
where n is the direction of the sea ice velocity relative to the iceberg,
W the iceberg width, and sigma_c = min(P, max(0, -n.sigma.n)) the
compressive sea ice stress normal to the iceberg face, limited by the sea
ice pressure P. The M |du| / dt limit stops the iceberg overshooting the
sea ice velocity.

  testcase                          stress (s11,s22,s12) N/m  P (N/m)  sea ice velocity  expected force
  seaice_stress_compression         (0, -2e4, 0)              3e4      0.5 m/s north     W * 2e4 = 1.33e7 N north
  seaice_stress_pressure_limit      (0, -2e4, 0)              1e4      0.5 m/s north     W * 1e4 = 6.7e6 N (pressure limited)
  seaice_stress_tension             (0, +2e4, 0)              3e4      0.5 m/s north     0 (tension)
  seaice_stress_perpendicular       (-2e4, 0, 0)              3e4      0.5 m/s north     0 (compression normal to the motion)
  seaice_stress_isotropic_diagonal  (-2e4, -2e4, 0)           3e4      0.5 m/s at 45 deg W * 2e4 along the motion
  seaice_stress_shear_diagonal      (0, 0, -2e4)              3e4      0.5 m/s at 45 deg W * 2e4 along the motion (n.sigma.n = sigma12)

In the compression tests the force is constant for the first 5 hours, is
reduced to M |du| / dt in hour 6 as the iceberg reaches the sea ice velocity,
and is zero after that, with the iceberg moving at 0.5 m/s.

Checks, all to rounding error:
  * no NaN or Inf in the output
  * sea ice stress at the iceberg equals the prescribed stress (tests the
    vertex averaging and interpolation to the iceberg)
  * the force at every step equals the expected force above, from the start
    of step iceberg velocity
  * momentum balance: M du / dt equals the sea ice force
  * the force on the sea ice is equal and opposite to the force on the iceberg
  * the iceberg never moves faster than the sea ice

As the stress is uniform these tests do not check the kite area weighting
used to average the corner stresses to vertices.


Grounding tests
---------------

grounding
  Setup:    simple grounding (config_iceberg_grounding_type = "simple").
            Air and ocean velocity 1 m/s north, air drag only, basal melt
            on (config_iceberg_basal_melt_coefficient = 5.0). A shallow band
            at 700 m depth between latitudes 3pi/8 -/+ pi/16, 1100 m
            elsewhere.
  Expected: the iceberg drifts north and stops on the step into the
            shallow band (day 59), where its draft (725 m) exceeds the depth.
            As basal melt reduces the draft it creeps slowly into the band
            (v below 0.05 m/s for several days), stopping again whenever its
            draft exceeds the local depth, then drifts freely across the band
            once the draft is below 700 m (day 70), leaving it at about day
            138. With daily output only the first stop shows as zero
            velocity.

grounding_force
  Setup:    as grounding but with force grounding
            (config_iceberg_grounding_type = "force").
  Expected: the iceberg drifts north and grounds on the step at day 59. The
            sediment force (9.3e4 N) holds it stationary for 3 days. Once
            basal melt has reduced the draft enough it breaks free (day 62)
            and drifts across the band, leaving it at about day 140. On this
            step bathymetry there is no bedrock contact, so the solid and
            gravity forces stay zero. results_grounding_force_forces.png
            shows the grounding force magnitudes.

Grounding force tests (grnd_*)
------------------------------

These test the individual force grounding terms
(config_iceberg_grounding_type = "force") following Kostov et al. (2026).
Only ocean drag and the selected grounding forces are active, with no basal
melt, so the iceberg draft is constant. The bathymetry is set relative to
the iceberg draft (828.5 m) through the "penetration", the draft minus the
ocean depth at the iceberg start:
  * 0 < penetration <= 8 m: scour into the sediment only (sediment force)
  * penetration > 8 m: bedrock contact with a freeboard anomaly of
    penetration - 8 m (solid and gravity forces)
using the default 8 m sediment depth, 90 m scour width, 6 kPa sediment shear
strength and 1800 kg/m^3 sediment density. "flat" bathymetry is uniform and
"ramp" bathymetry deepens northwards with the given slope. Runs are 5 days
with hourly output.

  testcase               forces on                 bathymetry      penetration  ocean v   expected
  grnd_none_ref          none (grounding "none")   deep (1100 m)   -            0.5 m/s   reference free drift
  grnd_none              sediment, gravity, solid  deep (1100 m)   -            0.5 m/s   all grounding forces exactly zero; identical to grnd_none_ref
  grnd_rest              sediment, gravity, solid  flat            20 m         0         no drivers: no NaN, iceberg stays at rest
  grnd_sediment_stuck    sediment                  flat            4 m          0.1 m/s   stays stuck; static sediment force equals the ocean drag (5.9e6 N), below its maximum (9.9e6 N)
  grnd_sediment_stop     sediment                  flat            20 m         0         starts at v = 0.1 m/s; sediment force (3.1e7 N) exceeds M v / dt (1.6e7 N) so it stops in one step
  grnd_sediment_partial  sediment                  flat            4 m          0.5 m/s   partial scour force 9.9e6 N; steady drift at 0.370 m/s where ocean drag balances it
  grnd_sediment_full_10  sediment                  flat            10 m         0.5 m/s   full 8 m scour force 3.1e7 N; steady drift 0.271 m/s
  grnd_sediment_full_20  sediment                  flat            20 m         0.5 m/s   identical to full_10: the scour depth is capped at 8 m
  grnd_solid             solid                     flat            8.01 m       0.5 m/s   solid force equals the buoyancy deficit force, 6.7e7 N; steady drift 0.162 m/s
  grnd_gravity           gravity                   ramp 2e-4       13 m         0         slides downslope (north) until bedrock contact ends; bathymetry gradient 1.98e-4 pointing south; force 6.6e6 N within 1% of expected
  grnd_solid_upslope     solid                     ramp 2e-6       8.15 m       -2 m/s    pushed upslope; freeboard anomaly grows until the solid force balances the drag; iceberg slows to near rest
  grnd_solid_downslope   solid                     ramp 2e-6       8.15 m       2 m/s     moves downslope; bedrock contact ends and the iceberg drifts freely at the ocean velocity
  grnd_all_ramp          sediment, gravity, solid  ramp 2e-4       7.58 m       1 m/s     moving downslope the solid force ends (step 2) before the sediment force (step 15), then free drift at the ocean velocity

Expected forces, with s the scour depth (penetration limited to 0-8 m) and a
the freeboard anomaly (penetration - 8 m, if positive):
  sediment  F = (1/2) g (rho_sed - rho_w) s^2 w + 2 tau s w + (1/sqrt(2)) tau s^2
  solid     F = M (rho_w / rho_i) (a / H) g cos(beta) cos(alpha)
  gravity   F = M (rho_w / rho_i) (a / H) g sin(beta) cos(beta), downslope
with w the scour width, tau the sediment shear strength, H the iceberg
height, beta the slope angle and alpha the trajectory angle relative to the
slope. The steady drift speed is where ocean drag balances the dissipative
force: v = v_ocean - sqrt(F / D).

grnd_all_ramp uses 7.58 m rather than 8.05 m because interpolating the ramp
bathymetry to the iceberg position adds about 0.47 m to the penetration for
a 2e-4 slope.

Checks for every test:
  * no NaN or Inf in the output
  * the dissipative (sediment and solid) forces oppose the iceberg motion
  * the iceberg velocity never reverses direction
plus the test specific checks listed above. On grounded (stopped) steps the
stored grounding force is the force needed to stop the iceberg, so the
iceberg momentum balance closes exactly on those steps.
